Day — Amazon ECR

Today I learned about Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) and how it is used to store and manage Docker container images in AWS.

I understood that building a Docker image locally is only the first step. To use that image with AWS services such as EKS, ECS, or Fargate, we need a container registry where the image can be stored and accessed securely. ECR provides that registry within AWS.

🔹 What I learned
ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is a managed AWS service for storing and managing container images.
An ECR Repository is where Docker images are stored.
Images can have different tags, such as latest, v1, or v2, to identify versions.
ECR integrates with IAM, allowing access to repositories to be controlled through AWS permissions.
ECR can be used with services like EKS, ECS, Fargate, and Lambda.
ECR also provides features such as image scanning and lifecycle policies for managing container images.
🔹 Hands-on Practice

I created an ECR repository and connected my local Docker environment with AWS using the AWS CLI.

I created a simple Docker image from an HTML/CSS website and practiced the complete process:

Dockerfile
    ↓
docker build
    ↓
Docker Image
    ↓
docker tag
    ↓
Amazon ECR
    ↓
docker push

I also learned why the image needs to be tagged with the ECR repository URI before pushing it to AWS.

🔹 Key Takeaway

The main thing I understood today is:

Docker builds the image → ECR stores the image → EKS/ECS can pull and run the image.

This hands-on practice helped me understand how container images move from a local development environment into AWS and how ECR fits into a real-world DevOps workflow.